1086	The combined point and line measurements of the previous two sections are next
1087	merged into a single measurement returning a pair of global polynomials that
1088	encode the $X$-basis and the $Z$-basis information simultaneously. This is
1089	done by encoding the pair as one polynomial in $2m+2$ variables and invoking
1090	the quantum soundness of the classical low individual degree test
1091	(Theorem~\ref{lem:ld-soundness}). The classical game, its question
1092	distribution, and the line-point distribution are defined at dimension $2m+2$
1093	only when $2m+2$ divides $q$ (Definition~\ref{def:ld-game}), a condition that
1094	admissibility of $(q,m,d)$ does not supply; the statements below are the
1095	source statements, without that condition, and their proofs are blocked at
1125		$f \in \deg_{md}(\ell_X)$ and $g \in \deg_{md}(\ell_Z)$, the polynomial
1126		$\mathrm{combine}_{f,g} \in \deg_{md+1}(\ell)$ is defined by
1127		\begin{equation}
1128			\label{eq:combine-lines}
1129			\mathrm{combine}_{f,g}(u + t \cdot v)
1130			= (u_\alpha + t \cdot v_\alpha)\, f(u_X + t \cdot v_X)
1131			+ (u_\beta + t \cdot v_\beta)\, g(u_Z + t \cdot v_Z)\,,
1132			\qquad t \in \F_q\,.
1133		\end{equation}
