1360			lying on $\ell$, it holds that $u_X \in \ell_X$ and
1361			$u_Z \in \ell_Z$. Moreover, the joint distribution of
1362			$(\ell_X, \ell_Z, u)$, for $(\ell, \ell_X, \ell_Z) \sim D$ and
1363			$u$ chosen uniformly at random on $\ell$, is a mixture of
1364			components indexed by a type
1365			$\mathrm{v} \in \{\mathrm{ALine}, \mathrm{DLine}\}$ and indices
1366			$i, j \in \{0, \dots, m-1\}$ with the following two properties
1367			within the component $(\mathrm{v}, i, j)$: the pair
1368			$(\ell_X, \ell_Z)$ is distributed according to the product of the
1369			marginal distributions on lines of $D_{\mathrm{v},i}$ and of
1370			$D_{\mathrm{v},j}$, and, conditioned on $(\ell_X, \ell_Z)$, the
1371			point $u_X$ is uniformly distributed on $\ell_X$ and likewise the
1372			point $u_Z$ is uniformly distributed on $\ell_Z$. Equivalently,
1373			since under each restricted distribution the point is uniformly
1374			distributed on the line conditionally on the line: within the
1375			component $(\mathrm{v}, i, j)$, the pair
1376			$((\ell_X, u_X), (\ell_Z, z'))$, where $z'$ is a uniformly random
1377			point of $\ell_Z$ sampled independently of $u$, is distributed
1378			according to $D_{\mathrm{v},i} \times D_{\mathrm{v},j}$, and
1379			symmetrically the pair $((\ell_X, x'), (\ell_Z, u_Z))$, with $x'$
1380			a uniformly random point of $\ell_X$ sampled independently of
1381			$u$, is distributed according to
1382			$D_{\mathrm{v},i} \times D_{\mathrm{v},j}$. (No assertion is made
1383			about the joint conditional law of the pair $(u_X, u_Z)$.)
1384			\item If $\ell$ is axis-parallel, then $\ell_X$ and $\ell_Z$ are
