1392			def:line-point-dist, def:ith-restricted-line, def:line,
1393			prop:line-equiv}
1394		The distribution $D$ is described by a sampling procedure. First sample
1395		$(\ell, u)$ from the line-point distribution over $\F_q^{2m+2}$ and write
1396		$u = (u_X, u_Z, \alpha, \beta)$, so that Property~1 holds by
1397		construction; all auxiliary randomness introduced below is sampled
1398		independently of $(\ell, u)$. As in
1399		Definition~\ref{def:ith-restricted-line}, write $\bar\chi$ for the
1400		zero-based shift of the index function at dimension $m$. Its classes
1401		$\bar\chi^{-1}(i)$ for $i \in \{0, \dots, m-1\}$ partition $\F_q$
1402		into sets of equal size $q/m$. Write $\bar\chi_{2m+2}$ for the analogous
1403		zero-based shift at dimension $2m+2$. For the extended-coordinate index in
1404		the construction below, write $k=r-1$ when the paper writes $r$; the
1405		source-line indices $i_X,i_Z$ are shifted in the same way. The function
1406		$\bar\chi_{2m+2}$ is defined only when $2m+2$ divides $q$, and hence so is
1407		the line-point distribution over $\F_q^{2m+2}$ from which $(\ell, u)$ is
1408		drawn
1409		(Definition~\ref{def:ld-question-distribution}); admissibility of
1410		$(q,m,d)$ supplies only that $m$ divides $q$, and for admissible
1411		parameters with $m \ge 2$ the required divisibility fails, so this
1412		first step is an unresolved obligation of the source argument, recorded
1413		in Remark~\ref{rem:qld-4-7-divisibility} and analyzed in
1414		\cite{gap:qpbt_ld-dimension-divisibility}; the construction below is
1415		carried out for the parameters for which the distribution is defined.
1416		By a
1417		\emph{fresh diagonal direction pair} we mean a pair $(s', w')$ with
1418		$s'$ uniformly random in $\F_q$ and $w' \in \F_q^m$ having coordinates
1419		$0, \dots, \bar\chi(s')-1$ equal to $0$ and the remaining coordinates
1420		uniformly random; this is exactly the law of the coordinate--direction
1421		data of the diagonal component of the line-point distribution over
1422		$\F_q^m$ (Definition~\ref{def:line-point-dist}).
1423	
1424		If $\ell$ is axis-parallel, $\ell = (v, e_k)$ with
1425		$v = (v_X, v_Z, \alpha_0, \beta_0)$ and $k \in \{0, \dots, 2m+1\}$, set
1426		$\ell_X = (v'_X, e_{i_X})$ and $\ell_Z = (v'_Z, e_{i_Z})$ where: for
1427		$0 \le k < m$, $i_X = k$ and $i_Z$ is uniformly random in
1428		$\{0,\dots,m-1\}$; for $m \le k < 2m$, $i_X$ is uniformly random and
1429		$i_Z = k - m$; for $2m \le k < 2m+2$, both $i_X$ and $i_Z$ are
1430		uniformly random; and $v'_X, v'_Z$ are the canonical line
1431		representatives of $v_X, v_Z$ for the chosen directions
1432		(Definition~\ref{def:line-representative}), so that $v_X \in \ell_X$
1433		and $v_Z \in \ell_Z$.
1434	
1435		If $\ell$ is diagonal, $\ell = (v, s, w)$, then by the construction of
1436		the diagonal component of the line-point distribution
1437		(Definition~\ref{def:ld-question-distribution} instantiated at
1438		dimension $2m+2$) the direction $w = (w_X, w_Z, \gamma, \delta)$ has
1439		coordinates $0, \dots, k-1$ equal to $0$, where
1440		$k = \bar\chi_{2m+2}(s)$,
1441		and its remaining coordinates are uniformly random. These remaining
1442		coordinates may also vanish, so $w$ need not have a nonzero coordinate
1443		at index $k$ and may be the zero vector, in which case $\ell$ is a
1444		singleton (Definition~\ref{def:line}).
1445		% In its one-based notation, the source proof describes w as "a direction
1446		% vector whose first nonzero coordinate is j = chi(s)"
1447		% (references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex, proof
1448		% of lem:qld-sublines); the distribution delivers only the weaker
1449		% property recorded here, which is the one the argument uses. The
1450		% source also leaves the laws of s_X and s_Z unspecified ("chosen such
1451		% that chi(s_X) = j" in its one-based notation); the uniform choices
1452		% below are needed for the exact marginals claimed in Property 2.
1453		Sample $\ell_X = (v'_X, s_X, w'_X)$ and $\ell_Z = (v'_Z, s_Z, w'_Z)$
1454		where: for $0 \le k < m$, $w'_X = w_X$ and $w'_Z = w_Z$, with $s_X$
1455		uniformly random in the class $\bar\chi^{-1}(k)$ and $s_Z$ uniformly
1456		random in the class $\bar\chi^{-1}(0)$; for $m \le k < 2m$, the pair
1457		$(s_X, w'_X)$ is a fresh diagonal direction pair and $w'_Z = w_Z$,
1458		with $s_Z$ uniformly random in the class $\bar\chi^{-1}(k-m)$; for
1459		$2m \le k < 2m+2$, both $(s_X, w'_X)$ and $(s_Z, w'_Z)$ are fresh
1460		diagonal direction pairs, sampled independently; and $v'_X, v'_Z$ are
1461		the canonical representatives of $v_X, v_Z$ for the directions
1462		$w'_X, w'_Z$.
1463	
1464		Property~3 is immediate from the procedure: when $\ell$ is
1465		axis-parallel, both $\ell_X$ and $\ell_Z$ are constructed to be
1466		axis-parallel. The containments in Property~2 hold for every point
1467		$u^\dagger = (u^\dagger_X, u^\dagger_Z, \alpha^\dagger,
1468		\beta^\dagger)$ of $\ell$: the block $u^\dagger_X$ differs from $v_X$
1469		by a multiple of the direction of $\ell_X$ when that direction is
1470		inherited from $\ell$ (namely $e_k$ for axis-parallel $\ell$ with
1471		$0 \le k < m$, and $w_X$ for diagonal $\ell$ with $0 \le k < m$),
1472		and equals $v_X$ in all other cases, since then the corresponding
1473		block of the direction of $\ell$ vanishes; in either case
1474		$u^\dagger_X \in \ell_X$ because $v_X \in \ell_X$, and symmetrically
1475		$u^\dagger_Z \in \ell_Z$.
1476	
1477		For the distributional part of Property~2, observe first that
1478		$(\ell_X, \ell_Z)$ is a function of $\ell$ and of the auxiliary
1479		randomness alone: the directions used are either read off from the
1480		direction data of $\ell$ or freshly sampled, and by
1481		Proposition~\ref{prop:line-equiv} the canonical representatives
1482		$v'_X, v'_Z$ do not depend on which base point of $\ell$ is used to
1483		compute them. Under both components of the line-point distribution the
1484		point $u$ is uniformly distributed on $\F_q^{2m+2}$ and, conditioned
1485		on the line $\ell$, uniformly distributed on the points of $\ell$
1486		(each of the $q$ values of the line parameter, or the single point
1487		when the direction vanishes, being equally likely). Hence, conditioned
1488		on $(\ell, \ell_X, \ell_Z)$, the sampled point $u$ is uniformly
1489		distributed on $\ell$, and it suffices to establish the claimed
1490		mixture description for the tuple $(\ell_X, \ell_Z, u)$ produced by
1491		the procedure itself.
1492	
1493		Condition on the component of the line-point distribution
1494		(axis-parallel or diagonal) and on $k$; the mixture of Property~2 is
1495		over these choices together with the fresh index choices, and the
1496		blocks $u_X, u_Z$ of $u$ are independent and uniform on $\F_q^m$,
1497		independent of all direction data. In the axis-parallel case with
1498		$0 \le k < m$, the pair $(\ell_X, u_X)$ consists of the uniformly
1499		random point $u_X$ together with the canonical axis-parallel line
1500		through it in direction $e_k$, so
1501		$(\ell_X, u_X) \sim D_{\mathrm{ALine},k}$, while, conditioned
1502		additionally on $i_Z$, the pair $(\ell_Z, u_Z)$ consists of the
1503		uniformly random point $u_Z$ together with the canonical line through
1504		it in direction $e_{i_Z}$, so
1505		$(\ell_Z, u_Z) \sim D_{\mathrm{ALine},i_Z}$; the two pairs are
1506		functions of the disjoint independent data $u_X$ and $(u_Z, i_Z)$,
1507		hence independent. In the diagonal case with $0 \le k < m$, the
1508		triple $(u_X, w_X, s_X)$ has $u_X$ uniform, $w_X$ with coordinates
1509		$0, \dots, k-1$ equal to $0$ and the remaining coordinates uniform,
1510		and $s_X$ uniform in $\bar\chi^{-1}(k)$, which is exactly the law
1511		generating $D_{\mathrm{DLine},k}$; hence
1512		$(\ell_X, u_X) \sim D_{\mathrm{DLine},k}$, and similarly
1513		$(\ell_Z, u_Z) \sim D_{\mathrm{DLine},0}$ since $w_Z$ is uniform and
1514		$s_Z$ is uniform in $\bar\chi^{-1}(0)$; the two pairs are functions of
1515		the disjoint independent data $(u_X, w_X, s_X)$ and $(u_Z, w_Z, s_Z)$,
1516		hence independent. The remaining cases are analogous: each of
1517		$(\ell_X, u_X)$ and $(\ell_Z, u_Z)$ is either built from the inherited
1518		direction block as above, or from a fresh axis index or fresh diagonal
1519		direction pair, in which case it is distributed as the uniform mixture
1520		over $i \in \{0, \dots, m-1\}$ of $D_{\mathrm{v},i}$ for the
1521		corresponding type $\mathrm{v}$; and the two pairs are always functions of
1522		disjoint independent collections of the variables $u_X$, $u_Z$, the
1523		direction blocks, and the fresh auxiliary choices, hence independent
1524		conditioned on the component. Within each component this yields exactly the
1525		description of Property~2: the pair $(\ell_X, \ell_Z)$ is
1526		product-distributed according to the line marginals of
1527		$D_{\mathrm{v},i}$ and $D_{\mathrm{v},j}$, and, conditioned on
1528		$(\ell_X, \ell_Z)$, each of $u_X$ and $u_Z$ is uniformly distributed
1529		on its line, because under each restricted distribution the point is
1530		uniform on the line conditionally on the line. The equivalent product
1531		formulations follow from the same conditional uniformity.
1532	\end{proof}
